{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "92",
    "case_summary": "A 49-year-old white man visited the Department of Surgical Sciences of the University of Insubria in January 2007 due to a palpable, painless nodule in the right breast, which he noticed three months prior. The patient had a family history of breast cancer, with his mother affected at the age of 55. His personal medical history did not reveal any significant findings. Physical examination identified a lump approximately 3 cm in size in the retroareolar region of the right breast. The lump had well-defined margins, a tense elastic consistency upon palpation, and was mobile without any changes to the skin or nipple-areola complex. No ipsilateral axillary lymph nodes were detected. Breast ultrasound revealed a solid mass measuring 3 × 1 cm with a homogeneous echostructure and well-defined margins in the right retroareolar region. Fine-needle aspiration showed only benign duct cells in the cytological specimens. Surgical resection of the lesion was performed in March 2007 for both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.\n\nThe lesion was composed of a proliferation of bland-looking cells admixed with thin collagen fibers. Cell appearance ranged from fibroblastic-like cells with elongated nuclei and scanty cytoplasm, to epitheliod-like oval cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and round to oval, centrally located, nuclei. No mitoses were found as well as areas of necrosis or hemorrhage. Immunohistochemical stains showed immunoreactivity for vimentin and CD34, while cells were completely negative for S100-protein, α-smooth muscle actin, desmin, cytoheratin AE1/AE3, and neurofilaments.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
